1.
(a) Identify the type of Operating System and justify.

Answer:
The suitable Operating System is a Multi-User Operating System (Linux).

Justification:
It allows multiple students to use the same computer system simultaneously.
It shares system resources such as CPU, memory, and storage efficiently.
It provides security by keeping each user's files and processes separate.
It supports multitasking and efficient process scheduling.

(b) State any two responsibilities of the kernel.

Answer:
Process Management: The kernel creates, schedules, and terminates processes while allocating CPU time.
Memory Management: The kernel allocates memory to processes and protects one process from accessing another process's memory.

(c) C-based system call sequence
#include<stdio.h>
#include<unistd.h>

int main()
{
    pid_t pid;

    pid = fork();

    if(pid == 0)
        printf("Child Process ID = %d\n", getpid());
    else
        printf("Parent Process ID = %d\n", getpid());

    return 0;
}

2.
(a) List the system calls.

Answer:
Operation	System Call
File Creation	creat() or open()
Writing	write()
Reading	read()
Closing	close()

(b) Explain any two system calls.
Answer:
open()
Opens an existing file or creates a new file.
Returns a file descriptor used for further operations.
write()
Writes data from memory into a file.
Returns the number of bytes written.

(c) C-based system call sequence
#include<fcntl.h>
#include<unistd.h>

int main()
{
    int fd;

    fd = creat("student.txt",0644);

    write(fd,"John",4);

    close(fd);

    return 0;
}
3.
(a) List the required system calls.

Answer:
open()
read()
write()
close()

(b) Explain any two.
Answer:
read()
Reads data from a file into memory.
Returns the number of bytes read.
write()
Writes data from memory into another file.
Used to copy file contents.

(c) C-based system call sequence
#include<fcntl.h>
#include<unistd.h>

int main()
{
    char buffer[100];

    int source = open("file1.txt",O_RDONLY);

    int dest = creat("file2.txt",0644);

    int n = read(source,buffer,100);

    write(dest,buffer,n);

    close(source);

    close(dest);

    return 0;
}
4.
(a) Purpose of fork() and exec().
Answer:
fork()
Creates a new child process by duplicating the parent process.
exec()
Replaces the current process with a new program.

(b) Explain wait().
Answer:
The wait() system call suspends the parent process until the child process completes execution. This prevents zombie processes and ensures proper synchronization.

(c) System call sequence
#include<stdio.h>
#include<unistd.h>
#include<sys/wait.h>

int main()
{
    pid_t pid = fork();

    if(pid==0)
    {
        execl("/bin/ls","ls",NULL);
    }
    else
    {
        wait(NULL);
        printf("Child Completed");
    }

    return 0;
}
5.
(a) Importance of file management.
Answer:
Organizes files and directories efficiently.
Enables easy storage and retrieval of data.
Provides file security and access control.
Prevents data loss through proper management.
Supports efficient use of secondary storage.

(b) Purpose of open() and close().
Answer:
open()
Opens a file for reading, writing, or both.
Returns a file descriptor.
close()
Closes the opened file.
Releases the file descriptor and associated system resources.

(c) C-based system call sequence
#include<fcntl.h>
#include<unistd.h>

int main()
{
    char buffer[100];

    int fd = open("student.txt",O_RDONLY);

    read(fd,buffer,100);

    close(fd);

    return 0;
}
